course: biology
lesson: 117
title: Zoos in Australia and around the world, and the year's myths put right
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Australian zoos often look after Australia's own odd and wonderful animals: Taronga in Sydney has giraffes with the Opera House behind them, Healesville bred the first platypus in a zoo, and Australia Zoo is the home of Steve Irwin's family and its wildlife hospital.
explain: Around the world zoos have their own stories: Singapore's Night Safari opens only in the dark, Berlin keeps one of the largest number of kinds of animals and had the famous polar bear Knut, Tokyo's Ueno Zoo is Japan's oldest, and in Ukraine keepers risked their lives to feed animals during the war.
explain: After a year of zoo visits, the notebook is full: big cats, apes, reptiles, birds, frogs and insects, keepers and vets, and a long list of cartoon «facts» that turned out to be myths.
explain: Elephants are not terrified of mice, lions do not chase mice for dinner, ostriches do not bury their heads, lemmings do not jump off cliffs on purpose, bats are not blind and goldfish remember for months: a good scientist checks a story before repeating it.
fact: Taronga Zoo in Sydney sits on a hill above the harbour; it runs a wildlife hospital and breeding programmes for Australian animals like the corroboree frog and the platypus.
fact: Australia Zoo in Queensland was made famous by Steve Irwin, «the Crocodile Hunter», who showed millions of people crocodiles and snakes on TV; his children Bindi and Robert still help run it and its wildlife hospital.
fact: Healesville Sanctuary near Melbourne keeps only Australian animals; it bred the first platypus in captivity in 1943 and helps save the helmeted honeyeater and Leadbeater's possum.
fact: Werribee near Melbourne and Monarto in South Australia are huge open-range zoos where herds of giraffes, rhinos and zebras roam large areas, more like a savanna.
fact: The southern corroboree frog, yellow and black, almost disappeared because of chytrid fungus; zoos breed it in very clean rooms and release eggs and young frogs into the mountains, and it makes its own poison, unlike poison dart frogs, which get theirs from the ants they eat.
fact: A platypus has a broad flat tail, and a koala has only a tiny stump of a tail hidden in its fur.
fact: Singapore Zoo uses moats and plants instead of bars, and its Night Safari, opened in 1994, shows nocturnal animals in the open at night.
fact: Berlin Zoo, opened in 1844, has one of the largest collections of animal species in the world, and its polar bear cub Knut became world-famous in 2007.
fact: During the war in Ukraine some zoos were reported to be shelled; keepers stayed to feed the animals or moved them to safety, and some animals were taken to zoos in Poland and other countries.
fact: Ueno Zoo in Tokyo opened in 1882 and is Japan's oldest zoo; giant panda twins born there in 2021 drew huge crowds.
fact: Myths put right this year: elephants are not afraid of mice (though any small sudden thing may startle them), lions do not hunt mice, ostriches do not bury their heads, lemmings do not jump off cliffs on purpose, bats are not blind and goldfish remember for months.
fact: The lemming myth was spread by a 1958 nature film in which the film-makers themselves drove lemmings off a cliff; real lemmings sometimes drown by accident when crowds of them cross water.
fact: Myths spread because they make good stories and people repeat them without checking; scientists test ideas with careful watching and experiments.
fact: Good zoo visitors stay on paths, do not tap the glass, do not feed the animals and read the signs, and some zoos run junior keeper days for children.
exercise: Which famous building can you see behind the giraffes at Taronga Zoo? | The Sydney Opera House | The Eiffel Tower | Big Ben | The Statue of Liberty
exercise: Who made Australia Zoo famous? | Steve Irwin, «the Crocodile Hunter» | George Mottershead, the founder of Chester Zoo | Bindi the koala | Captain Cook, the sailor who mapped Australia
exercise: What did Healesville Sanctuary do first, in 1943? | Bred a platypus in captivity | Opened the first night safari for visitors | Cloned a kangaroo | Built a cable car over the bush
exercise: What are Werribee and Monarto? | Huge open-range zoos in Australia | Two famous koalas from a children's TV show | Islands kept for corroboree frogs | Night zoos in Singapore
exercise: What almost wiped out the southern corroboree frog? | Chytrid fungus | Hungry crocodiles | Too much sunshine | Visitors collecting them
exercise: How is the corroboree frog different from poison dart frogs? | It makes its own poison instead of getting it from food | It has no poison at all, so any animal can eat it safely | It is blue and orange | It lives in the salty sea, not in ponds or wet moss
exercise: What is special about Singapore's Night Safari? | It shows nocturnal animals in the open at night | It is a zoo on a big boat that sails around the island | It only keeps owls | It is open only in winter, when the nights are longest
exercise: How does Singapore Zoo keep animals in without bars? | With moats and plants | With invisible magic walls | With loud music | It does not keep them in
exercise: Which polar bear cub at Berlin Zoo became world-famous in 2007? | Knut | Winnipeg | Paddington | Kurt
exercise: What did keepers in some Ukrainian zoos do during the war? | Stayed to feed animals or moved them to safety | Let every animal run free into the city streets | Closed the gates and left the animals behind | Sold the animals to circuses
exercise: Which is Japan's oldest zoo, opened in 1882? | Ueno Zoo in Tokyo | Taronga Zoo | Berlin Zoo | Singapore Zoo
exercise: Which of these is true, not a myth? | Goldfish can remember things for months | Bats are completely blind and see nothing | Ostriches bury their heads in sand when scared | Elephants are terrified of mice
exercise: Why do animal myths spread so easily? | They make good stories and people repeat them without checking | Scientists invent them on purpose to test how clever people are | Animals tell them to each other | They are printed on the back of zoo tickets
exercise: How do scientists check an animal «fact»? | Careful watching and experiments | Asking which story is the funniest | Seeing which cartoon shows it | Voting on it
exercise: What does a good zoo visitor do? | Stays on paths, reads signs and does not feed the animals | Climbs the fence for a closer look at the big cats | Taps the glass to wake the sleeping animals up | Shares lunch with the monkeys
exercise: What can children do on a junior keeper day? | Help keepers with their work for a day | Take an animal home | Ride the elephants | Feed visitors to the lions
test: In Dumbo the elephants scream and climb on stools when they see a mouse. What is right? | Elephants are not really terrified of mice; they may be startled by any small sudden thing | Elephants faint at the smell of mice | Mice live inside elephants' trunks, so elephants are always scared they will crawl up | Elephants hunt mice for food and chase them round the savanna, so the film shows it backwards
test: «lemmings jump off cliffs on purpose, saw it on telly» What is right? | They do not; an old film staged it, and lemmings drown only by accident while moving | Yes, they do it every spring when there are too many of them in the hills | Yes, but only male lemmings | Lemmings can fly a little, like flying squirrels, so they are fine
test: «the corroboree frog's poison comes from eating ants innit, like dart frogs» What is right? | No, it makes its own poison | Yes, it eats poison ants | It has no poison at all | It gets poison from the mud
test: Why must zoos keep corroboree frogs in very clean rooms? | So the chytrid fungus cannot reach them | So visitors can see them better | Because frogs dislike all water | So the frogs stay small
test: Why would a zoo open only at night? | To show nocturnal animals awake and active | Because it is cooler for the visitors to sleep | Because animals vanish in daylight | To save money on tickets
test: «Steve Irwin used to grab crocs on TV, good idea or nah» What is a fair answer? | It made millions care about animals, but handling wild animals is risky | It was totally safe for anyone to copy at home or at the zoo, no worries | It was bad for animals in every way | Crocodiles enjoyed being grabbed and would swim up to him to play
test: What would a clever zoo visitor do on hearing the next amazing animal «fact»? | Check it in a good book or with a scientist before repeating it | Repeat it to everyone straight away, because amazing facts are always true | Believe it if it is in a cartoon | Ignore every fact forever, even ones from good books
test: A zoo has space for one big area or many small cages. Which is a strong reason to choose fewer kinds with more space? | Animals need space, choice and things to do to stay healthy | More kinds of animals always means happier animals | Small cages are better for breeding | Visitors prefer small cages
test: «Taronga had 6 giraffes but, wait, one's a baby born yesterday so 7. how many legs» | 28, four legs each for 7 giraffes | 24, four legs each for 6 giraffes | 14, two legs each for 7 giraffes | 21, three legs each
test: «2 platypuses and 3 koalas at Healesville, how many tails, mate» | 5, one tail each, though a koala's is tiny | 10, two tails each for all five of them | 2, koalas have no tail at all | 3, platypuses have no tail, only flat feet
test: «Manny in Ice Age has one trunk and two massive tusks, real mammoths same?» What is right? | Yes, one trunk and two long curved tusks, like a hairy elephant | No, real mammoths had two trunks | No, real mammoths had no tusks at all | No, real mammoths had four tusks and no trunk
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Healesville Sanctuary? | It maintains solely Australian fauna and first bred the platypus in captivity in 1943 | It is a nocturnal zoo situated in Singapore | It is the oldest zoological institution in Japan, having been established in the year 1882 | It exhibits exclusively animals of African origin within extensive open-range enclosures
exam: Identify the principal cause of the decline of the southern corroboree frog. | Infection by chytrid fungus | Predation by released zoo crocodiles | Excessive collection by tourists | The construction of the Sydney Opera House
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dissemination of animal myths. | They are repeated because they constitute agreeable stories and are seldom verified | They are disseminated deliberately by scientific institutions | They are invariably correct when depicted in animated films | They originate exclusively in zoological gardens
exam: Ascertain the year in which Ueno Zoo, the oldest zoological institution in Japan, was opened to the public. | Eighteen hundred and eighty-two | Seventeen hundred and fifty-two | Eighteen hundred and twenty-eight | Nineteen hundred and ninety-four
